I accept SOL-HIGH-10’s open-path refutation: marked open owns structural validation, not an O(log) provider-agreement walk. The new boundary-pin test passes.

However, the broader convergence argument is unsound: `--deep` misses monotone below-mark coordinate tampering.

### SOL-HIGH-11 — MAJOR

[canonical_audit.py:562](/Users/kaygee/Code/loops/libs/engine/src/engine/canonical_audit.py:562), [canonical_audit.py:620](/Users/kaygee/Code/loops/libs/engine/src/engine/canonical_audit.py:620)

Deep audit orders index rows by `(arrival_ordinal, arrival_seq)`, but selects and compares only `FACT_COLUMNS`/`TICK_COLUMNS`, which explicitly exclude the coordinates. Consequently, changing coordinates while preserving row order passes field-for-field comparison.

Empirical reproduction on three facts at `(1,0), (2,0), (3,0)`:

```sql
UPDATE facts
SET arrival_ordinal = 1, arrival_seq = 1
WHERE arrival_ordinal = 2;
```

Results:

```text
coordinates before: [(1, 0), (2, 0), (3, 0)]
coordinates after:  [(1, 0), (1, 1), (3, 0)]
marked public reopen: accepted
L1 ok: True
deep ok: True
prefix <= ordinal 1 before: [0]
prefix <= ordinal 1 after:  [0, 1]
```

This meets all three requested conditions:

- Marked public open accepts it; the schema remains NOT NULL and UNIQUE.
- L1 misses it because no row exceeds consumed ordinal 3; deep misses it because row order and content remain unchanged.
- It corrupts a consumer-visible claim: [StoreReader.facts_by_kind:687](/Users/kaygee/Code/loops/libs/engine/src/engine/store_reader.py:687) defines `at_ordinal=1` as the prefix received through log ordinal 1, but now includes a fact actually issued at ordinal 2.

The new test at [test_audit_rebase_d3.py:256](/Users/kaygee/Code/loops/libs/engine/tests/test_audit_rebase_d3.py:256) only covers the above-mark `+100` class caught by `rewound`; it does not pin the asserted below-mark deep-audit boundary.

Deep audit needs to compare each stored coordinate with `(record["ord"], expansion_seq)`, not merely use coordinates for ordering.

